  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.

  Presence stub for com.google.android.googlequicksearchbox ("the Google app",
  internally Velvet). Android Auto refuses to start without a package by this
  name installed. This package satisfies that check and nothing else: it holds
  no permissions, declares no components, and contains no code.

  Deliberately absent, and why:

    * No <activity>, <service>, <provider> or <receiver>. The real Google app
      owns the assistant, the voice-search UI, the "Ok Google" hotword and a
      handful of implicit intents. A stub that declared any of those would be
      selected by the system as the handler for them and would then do nothing,
      which is worse than not being installed at all. On /e/OS the assistant
      slot is either empty or held by something the user chose; leave it alone.

    * No <uses-permission>. If this package ever needs a permission, something
      has gone wrong with the design.

    * No android:sharedUserId, no android:process. Nothing to share and nothing
      to run.

  android:forceQueryable is honoured by the framework only for packages
  installed on a system partition (see stubs/README.md). It is harmless when
  it is ignored, and load-bearing when this is built into a ROM.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.google.android.googlequicksearchbox"
    android:versionCode="1"
    android:versionName="0.1.0-openaap-stub">

    <uses-sdk android:minSdkVersion="33" android:targetSdkVersion="35" />

    <application
        android:label="Google app (Android Auto compatibility stub)"
        android:hasCode="false"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:forceQueryable="true" />

</manifest>
